WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.780
Generally speaking, if you hock something
at a pawn shop, you're desperate for money.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.360
And that's exactly what the Rick, Corey, and
the gang at the World Famous Gold &amp; Silver

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.860
Pawn Shop in Las Vegas are counting on when
they try to buy things at ridiculously low

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:13.360
prices.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:17.400
Here's a look at some of the times the Pawn
Stars have seriously cheated customers.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.160
Isaac Newton's book

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.100
In season three of Pawn Stars, a seller named
Bob entered the Pawn Shop with a priceless

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.920
artifact: one of the few remaining books from
the library of Sir Isaac Newton.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.619
After rare books expert Gary verified it was
authentic, estimating a retail value of around

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:37.800
$20,000, the Old Man delivered an insultingly
lowball offer of just $5,000.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.620
Bob talked him up to $7,000, even offering
to throw in a free dinner if the Old Man agreed.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.960
He did, and probably laughed all the way to
the bank.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:46.840
Hotchkiss Cannon

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.900
When Rick asked his weapons expert how much
he could pay for an 1890 Hotchkiss Cannon

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.920
and still make money, this was the answer.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.460
"I would say $40000 all day long"

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.300
But even though Chuck, the seller, was standing
right there and heard Rick could pay $40,000

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.140
for it and still make money, he still decided
to offer the cannon for just $30,000.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:11.440
Rick agreed, and after testing to make sure
the cannon still fired, Chuck got to see all

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.080
his potential profits go up in smoke.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.820
1984 Chris Craft Boat

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.640
In a very early episode of the series, Corey
takes a supposedly huge gamble on buying a

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.600
1984 Chris Craft boat for $16,500 before having
an expert look it over.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.600
As has become the regular schtick of the show,
both Rick and the Old Man hem and haw at how

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:34.470
much Corey spent on such a risky purchase...
until it's later revealed that with just $4,000

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.880
of restoration work, the boat has an approximate
retail value of $30,000.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.840
Considering the seller took an $8500 loss
when he sold the boat, that had to really

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.400
sting.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.360
1962 Lincoln Continental

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.520
In another early episode from the first season,
Rick and the Old Man make a steal of a deal

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:53.260
on a restored 1962 Lincoln Continental.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:59.080
The seller, Devin, says he wants to get $13,000
to $14,000 for it, but eventually parts ways

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.020
with it for just $9,500.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.420
You can guess where this is going.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:07.040
After pouring another 15 grand into restorations,
Rick and the Old Man were left with a car

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:13.269
valued between "$30,000 to $35,000," meaning
Devin's potential loss translated directly

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:17.900
to a possible $5,500 to $10,500 gain for the
Pawn Stars.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.300
Samurai sword

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.299
In what is probably the most egregious example
of the guys knowingly underpaying for an item,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.860
Corey bilked an unsuspecting lawyer out of
a 15th century samurai sword that a client

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:29.860
had left as collateral.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.040
Just before the haggling began, Corey admitted
in a backroom confessional that he has "seen

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:38.870
a few of these sell for thousands of dollars,"
which makes his initial offer of $800 laughably

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:39.370
shady.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:44.320
Eventually, they agreed on a price of $1,500
for a weapon later appraised to be worth between

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.580
5 and 6 grand, with a potential value of up
to 15 thousand dollars with a little restoration.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:50.640
Yikes.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.040
Autographed Godfather screenplay

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.500
One of the weirdest cases of the Pawn Stars
trying to rip someone off and failing miserably

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.500
came when a woman tried to sell them a copy
of The Godfather screenplay signed by "Al."

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:05.800
After their handwriting expert authenticated
the signature as belonging to star Al Pacino,

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:07.360
Rick… well….

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.140
"I'm gonna make him an offer he can't refuse."

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.280
...except she refused his offer for a measly
$500.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:19.020
Instead, she walked away, and later sold it
for $12,000 at a charity fundraiser.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.200
The twist ending?

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.719
It later turned out the screenplay wasn't
signed by Al Pacino at all, but rather Godfather

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.680
producer Al Ruddy, meaning the Pawn Stars
actually got lucky when their own attempt

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:29.700
to bilk someone failed.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.800
1951 Yankees autographed baseball

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.359
Finally, a would-be seller wisely avoided
being another ripoff victim when the gang

00:03:37.359 --> 00:03:42.860
at Pawn Stars offered him a crummy $800 for
a baseball signed by the 1951 World Series

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.420
Champion New York Yankees.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:50.099
Instead, he walked away, which is a good thing,
because in 2016, a similar ball sold at auction

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.860
for over $6000.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.260
Thanks for watching!

